  "provider_query": "Python standard library documentation for the venv module",
  "answer": "Official and closely related sources for Python’s standard-library **`venv`** module:\n\n1. **`venv` — Creation of virtual environments** (library reference, Python 3.14) — https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/venv.html  \n   Main stdlib page: purpose, CLI (`python -m venv`), options, and the `EnvBuilder` API. Added in Python 3.3. [web:2]\n\n2. **Latest library reference (3.x)** — https://docs.python.org/3/library/venv.html  \n   Same page on the current stable docs track. [web:8]\n\n3. **Tutorial: Virtual Environments and Packages** (Python 3 docs) — https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/venv.html  \n   Practical guide: create with `python -m venv`, common `.venv` layout, and activation on Windows and Unix. [web:0]\n\n4. **Tutorial source (CPython)** — https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/HEAD/Doc/tutorial/venv.rst  \n   Upstream reStructuredText for the tutorial section. [web:4]\n\n5. **venv overview (pydevtools handbook)** — https://pydevtools.com/handbook/reference/venv/  \n   Concise summary of the built-in module (since 3.3), comparison with virtualenv and uv, and basic creation commands. [web:1]\n\n6. **How to use different Python versions with stdlib `venv`** — https://www.pythontutorials.net/blog/how-do-i-use-different-python-version-in-venv-from-standard-library-not-virtualenv/  \n   Notes that the interpreter you invoke (`python3.12 -m venv …`) determines the environment’s Python; mentions the `python3.x-venv` package on some Linux installs. [web:5]\n\n7. **Python Land: create, activate, deactivate, delete** — https://python.land/virtual-environments/virtualenv  \n   Walkthrough of `python -m venv` and typical directory layout; links to the official docs for full options. [web:6]\n\n8. **ZetCode: Python venv** — https://zetcode.com/python/venv/  \n   Short tutorial on creating and activating environments and what `pyvenv.cfg` and `site-packages` are for. [web:7]",
